WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.379
So far, you've used fast
rules-based evaluations that

2
00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:05.740
are suitable for running
frequently on every commit.

3
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.109
For stages of development
leading up to deployment,

4
00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:12.410
more robust and comprehensive
evaluation methods can help you better

5
00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.699
ensure overall quality before
you deploy the app to users.

6
00:00:16.579 --> 00:00:18.989
One such method is
model-graded evaluation,

7
00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:23.000
where you'll use an LLM
to evaluate the LLM app.

8
00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:25.780
Let's take a look at this
kind of eval and how to

9
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.699
automate this as part of
your testing pipeline.

10
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:32.659
OK, until now, we've been
using rules-based evals

11
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:34.979
to make sure our models
follow the guidelines

12
00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:39.420
we set up in our prompt and stick to
the facts we provided in our data set.

13
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.020
But in order to have full
confidence in our application,

14
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.189
we also need to be sure our
model is generating high-quality,

15
00:00:48.189 --> 00:00:49.320
contextually appropriate responses.

16
00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:52.240
Evaluating LLM output
can be tricky because

17
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.899
a good response to a query is subjective.

18
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.420
We could try and write custom
rules like we did for our initial

19
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.880
evals to make sure that
expected data is in the output,

20
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.700
but this gets more and
more complicated and more

21
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.900
and more fragile as the application grows.

22
00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:13.680
One approach to checking the output of
an LLM is to use another LLM as a grader.

23
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.420
This is referred to as
model graded evaluation.

24
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.120
We'll show a quick
example to make sure our model

25
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.180
is actually producing output as a quiz.

26
00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:25.099
We aren't concerned
with the content just yet,

27
00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:29.920
only that the LLM is giving back
responses that look like a set of quiz questions.

28
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.400
We'll look more closely at the
quality of output in our next lesson.

29
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.120
So, in this lesson, we're
going to focus on judging

30
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.689
whether the response is
in the desired format.

31
00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:41.629
And then, in our next lesson,

32
00:01:41.629 --> 00:01:44.200
we'll look at things like
hallucination and adherence.

33
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.930
So, let's jump right in,
and write a passing and

34
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.840
a failing test case to see how this works.

35
00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:55.129
First off we need to
establish our keys again as we

36
00:01:55.129 --> 00:01:58.159
did in the previous
lesson so we'll do that now.

37
00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:09.039
For the purposes of
adding model graded evals,

38
00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.659
we're going to continue to use the
application that we generated in the last lesson.

39
00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:17.139
So, let's take another look at that
just to remember what we're working on. 

40
00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:19.879
Again, you can use the cat
command if you want to see the

41
00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:22.879
contents of one of these
files on your local file system.

42
00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:25.979
They're all included with
the lab that you have. Now,

43
00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:30.139
let's look at what it looks
like to build a model graded eval.

44
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.650
This is going to look similar to the work
that we did to build the quiz assistant,

45
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.379
except in this case, we are
building a prompt that tells

46
00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:43.759
the LLM to evaluate the
output of the quiz assistant.

47
00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:49.129
So, you can see here that we're giving
specific instructions to the LLM to tell

48
00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:53.620
it the role that it plays in
evaluating the work of the quiz assistant.

49
00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:56.259
Before evaluating an
actual quiz assistant,

50
00:02:56.259 --> 00:03:00.030
we are going to simulate
this by writing an LLM response

51
00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.110
as if it came from the quiz assistant and

52
00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.199
using our eval to
determine if that response would

53
00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:08.939
pass the test that we're looking to make.

54
00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:13.740
As you can see here, the
full message or prompt

55
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.030
for the eval is telling
the LLM to evaluate a

56
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.469
generated quiz based on
the context and determine

57
00:03:21.469 --> 00:03:25.759
whether or not it
looks like a quiz or test.

58
00:03:26.259 --> 00:03:30.120
It is not meant to evaluate
whether the information is correct.

59
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:36.020
Then, it is told specifically to
output a Y if the response is a quiz,

60
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.520
and N if the response
does not look like a quiz.

61
00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.770
Now, we are going to use
langchain to build the familiar

62
00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.099
chain that we saw in the previous lesson,

63
00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:49.580
except this time we're building a
chain specifically to do the evaluation.

64
00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:53.840
So first, we have our chat prompt
template as we built previously. 

65
00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.039
Next, we select our LLM, which
also we did in the previous lesson. 

66
00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.620
And again, we're using GPT 3.5 Turbo.
And finally, we select an output parser,

67
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.319
which again is the STR output
parser to take the response

68
00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:09.919
from the LLM and generate a basic string.

69
00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.699
We chain these together as we
did previously, but to recap,

70
00:04:13.699 --> 00:04:17.129
we take the eval prompt,
we pipe it to the LLM,

71
00:04:17.129 --> 00:04:20.240
we take that response
and pipe it to the output

72
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.040
parser to get the string
that we're looking for.

73
00:04:23.439 --> 00:04:29.060
So now, we've built a basic eval
chain using a known good LLM response,

74
00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:45.819
And as you can see in
this case, we get a Y,

75
00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:48.939
which means the known good
response that we put in is believed

76
00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:52.319
by the LLM to look like a quiz
in the format that's expected.

77
00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:57.639
However, we also want to ensure that it
will fail if it doesn't look like a quiz.

78
00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:02.600
In order to do that, we'll first
store all of the eval chain creation

79
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.620
code as a utility function
so we can use it repeatedly.

80
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:11.399
Next, we store a known bad result so
that we can pass it into a new eval chain.

81
00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.750
And now, we invoke this new eval chain
and see that we get the correct response,

82
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.639
which is an N to indicate that the
text does not look like a quiz. Now,

83
00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:27.519
we're gonna take our newly created model
graded eval capability and incorporate it

84
00:05:27.519 --> 00:05:31.459
into the tests that we're running inside
of our continuous integration pipeline.

85
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.240
So, we have two files for testing
on the file system in the lab. 

86
00:05:36.839 --> 00:05:39.939
The first is
testassistant.py which you can see

87
00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:43.040
here and is what we
were running previously.

88
00:05:43.519 --> 00:05:49.689
The second is test-release-evals.py which
is a roll-up of all of the work we just

89
00:05:49.689 --> 00:05:55.439
did to show how to create a
model-graded eval and execute it against OpenAI.

90
00:05:56.079 --> 00:06:00.920
Okay, so now that we have the
release evals which are the model-graded

